{
  "markdown_report": "**Insights from the starting point.** (1) Positive-edge coverage is only 1.86–4.25%. Transition evidence therefore reaches few sampled training targets. (2) Deployment/training residual MAE is approximately 53–126% of mean absolute residual magnitude. This motivates testing graph alignment, but does not establish ranking harm. (3) Qualitative feedback questions broad historical associations. Histories alone cannot establish current intent, ownership, compatibility, or satisfied needs.\n\n**Insights from related work.** (1) Sampled evaluation can reverse model comparisons [Dallmann et al.](https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.13045). Keep the pinned selection criterion and separate full-catalog observations. (2) Plausible explanations may misrepresent influential evidence [Walk the Talk?](https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/hash/b5ec50eb177908f21f78ed0d76ed525c-Abstract-Conference.html). Simulator narratives generate hypotheses, not preference labels. (3) Metamorphic testing detects implementation faults [Michiels et al., 2024](https://doi.org/10.1145/3591109). Predictor parity should precede performance interpretation. (4) [Official SASRec](https://github.com/kang205/SASRec/blob/master/model.py) uses raw embedding-dot-product logits without a transition branch. Historical residual diagnostics do not establish that the starting model contains one.\n\n**Framework and directions.** Separate evidence coverage, ranking influence, validation benefit, and implementation correctness.\n\n| Direction | Existing measurement | Missing evidence | Proposed intervention |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Evidence availability | Edge coverage | Benefit outside supported targets | Support shrinkage |\n| Ranking influence | Raw residual magnitude | Boundary crossings | Residual scale audit |\n| Temporal relevance | Qualitative feedback | Dependence on older events | History dropout |\n| Implementation correctness | Compatibility interface | Actual branch and export fidelity | Architecture checks and parity tests |\n\n**Candidate ideas.** Scores are originality/potential/difficulty, each out of ten; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n| Idea | Scores | Positive and negative assessment |\n|---|---|---|\n| Residual scale calibration | 3/6/4 | Originality: connects deployment diagnostics with selection; scalar calibration is established. Potential: cheaply isolates branch utility; cannot repair missing edges. Difficulty: scoring change is small; branch verification, history access, and export require care. |\n| Distinct-user support shrinkage | 4/6/5 | Originality: connects reliability to residual strength; shrinkage is established. Potential: suppresses fragile associations; sparse coverage may erase benefits. Difficulty: compact scoring rule; support counting and fold alignment complicate implementation. |\n| Training history dropout | 2/6/4 | Originality: tests dependence on isolated events; regularization is familiar. Potential: may improve robustness; can remove useful recent evidence. Difficulty: small training change; retraining consumes budget. |\n\n**Selected idea: deployment-consistent residual scale calibration.** At 0% progress, prioritize a cheap falsifiable intervention, conditional on an existing residual-bearing checkpoint. Expose `s_base + alpha * residual`, retaining the existing gate inside `residual`. Freeze `{0, 0.5, 1}` before results; use one checkpoint and identical pinned validation candidates. Select by macro validation HR@10, with ties favoring the largest alpha. Report category changes and top-10 turnover. This estimates checkpoint-specific inference utility; co-adaptation prevents architectural conclusions without matched retraining. Positive temperature scaling alone cannot change HR@10.\n\nVerify the branch, history access, checkpoint restoration, and alpha=1 reconstruction first. If absent, mark calibration unavailable; adding a branch is a separate experiment. Retain the diagnostic API, restore module modes, report missing measurements explicitly, and verify exported-predictor parity. Keep full-catalog unseen-item lists for the twenty users descriptive. Preserve all four routes, seeds, splits, environment paths, epoch settings, and budget accounting. Log every alpha comparison: validation selection can overfit, and popularity-based shortcuts remain possible.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Conditionally calibrate an existing candidate-dependent transition branch using score_alpha = base_score + alpha * gated_transition_residual. Compare alpha values 0, 0.5, and 1 using one frozen checkpoint and the pinned validation benchmark. First establish that the actual local architecture, checkpoint, and generated predictor contain and restore the branch. Standard SASRec has no such branch; historical diagnostic output alone is insufficient evidence. If absent, report this idea as unavailable rather than introducing a new branch under the same experimental label.",
    "motivation": "Measured positive-edge coverage is low, and deployment/training residual discrepancies are large relative to residual magnitude. Neither observation establishes ranking harm: small candidate-dependent changes can matter near the top-10 boundary. A controlled scale intervention tests the deployed branch's incremental utility for a particular checkpoint without converting qualitative feedback into preference labels. At early research progress, this conditional, inexpensive experiment remains useful, but it must not be presented as an immediately applicable modification to standard SASRec.",
    "implementation_notes": "Preserve the exact base score and existing gate; alpha multiplies the complete deployed residual, avoiding double gating. Verify the architecture prerequisite from the actual implementation and checkpoint during host-side implementation. Do not infer a residual from aggregate complete-minus-raw margins. Require an explicit architecture-specific scoring hook receiving history or explicitly retained branch context: predict_score(features, items) alone does not guarantee the necessary information. Do not use mutable last-history caches, which can corrupt batched or interleaved inference. Preserve the compatibility interface through a documented adapter to the same complete deployed scoring path.\n\nFreeze alpha_grid=[0.0,0.5,1.0], one checkpoint, and the tie rule before obtaining validation results. Use the baseline checkpoint selection policy unchanged; do not select a different epoch for each alpha. If training is required, retain preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, and the environment-controlled SASREC_EPOCHS default of 200. Compare identical histories, exclusion masks, validation users, candidate IDs, and evaluator tie handling. Select one global alpha by equal-weight macro validation HR@10; break exact ties toward the largest alpha. Do not fit category-specific scales in this experiment. A positive global temperature or candidate-independent offset preserves rankings and cannot itself improve HR@10. Use raw logits without adding a sigmoid to prediction.\n\nBefore validation, require alpha=1 score reconstruction and checkpoint-reload/model/export parity on deterministic public training inputs. Check candidate-permutation equivariance, batch-versus-single consistency, finite outputs, correct category/item routing, and chunked-versus-unchunked scoring. Use float32 comparisons with rtol=1e-5 and atol=1e-6; require matching top-10 outputs under the same tie rule and investigate discrepancies before proceeding. Include short, padded, and maximum-length histories. For causal SASRec, changing future tokens must not alter earlier features; history-order invariance is not a required property. These are adaptations of recommendation testing principles, not asserted built-in RecPack Tests checks.\n\nRecord every alpha's per-category and macro validation HR@10. Where the host exposes paired outcomes or scores, also report top-10 turnover, target-rank changes, boundary gaps, and paired user-hit changes. Otherwise mark those validation diagnostics unavailable; compute explicitly labeled training-side sensitivity separately without changing the evaluator. One changed hit among 200 users in one category changes macro HR@10 by 0.00125. Do not fabricate uncertainty intervals when paired outcomes are absent. Any intervals condition on the fixed candidates and training seed and do not account for adaptive selection. Selecting alpha on validation can overfit; no selected validation gain establishes held-out improvement.\n\nRetain DiagnosisProbe(view, device), run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), and diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis). Use view.model as the actual trained module; preserve view.item_emb, log2feats, predict_score, user_train, meta_data, and review_data. Process at most four batches and the first 512 valid supervised positions per batch, excluding padding. Use evaluation mode and no_grad; restore every submodule's original training flag in finally, including on exceptions. Preserve existing pairwise and geometry measurements, add branch-supported ranking sensitivity, and return metrics and metric_definitions with observation counts, units, signs, and null plus reasons for unavailable measurements. Explain that raw margins depend on score scale and mean embedding cosine alone does not establish collapse. Keep unavailable item-category breakdowns empty. The interpreter prompt must embed measured metrics and definitions, distinguish training sensitivity from validation benefit, and prohibit invented metadata or latent traits.\n\nKeep graph construction training-only. For an existing user-excluding or cross-fitted branch, preserve its established training and deployment policies during the alpha sweep. Excluding each user's entire history prevents that user's interactions from contributing to their training graph features, but deployment graph support, normalization, and residual scale can still differ. Record those policies and retain the observed shift diagnostic. Changing graph construction or aligning training/deployment graphs is a separate experiment. Fixed-checkpoint alpha=0 does not substitute for training a branch-free baseline: backbone and residual may co-adapt. Architectural claims require independently trained matched models with equal tuning opportunities and accounted costs.\n\nSave alpha, score decomposition version, architecture configuration, checkpoint identity, graph artifacts and construction policy, item mappings, and category routing beneath the trial directory. Update the predictor writer so working/predict.py reconstructs the trained architecture and selected scoring path; make diagnostic default scores use the same alpha. Separately produce the prescribed full-catalog unseen-item lists for twenty real training users, five per category, using training histories and chunked scoring. Without relevance labels, these lists support descriptive concentration, coverage, and stability only. Train-only popularity and history-length strata may reveal shortcuts but cannot establish current intent or causal preference.\n\nRetain Movies_and_TV, Industrial_and_Scientific, Electronics, and CDs_and_Vinyl; DATA_DIR = os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR']; unchanged splits; and the pinned host evaluator with 200 validation users/category, 99 unique negatives, and evaluation seed 42. Use single-GPU execution and record training, diagnostics, debugging, failures, and all comparison costs against the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget. Freeze a remaining-budget cap before execution; do not launch optional retraining without enough budget. Use only public histories, titles, descriptions, and timestamps, with no external data or pretrained weights. Ignore unavailable ratings, review text, prices, stores, item categories, and simulator traits. Read no hidden tests. If the branch prerequisite fails, stop this calibration experiment, preserve the valid SASRec predictor, and report the missing prerequisite; history dropout remains a separately registered candidate rather than an unreported fallback.",
    "pseudocode": "freeze alpha_grid = [0.0, 0.5, 1.0], tie_rule = largest_alpha\nfreeze checkpoint policy, artifact manifest, and remaining GPU-time cap\nobtain prescribed baseline checkpoint without changing splits or seeds\nif actual model/checkpoint/predictor lack an existing transition branch:\n    record calibration_unavailable with reason\n    preserve valid baseline predictor\n    stop this experiment\nrequire explicit score_components(history, candidates)\nfreeze checkpoint and existing graph construction policies\nbase, gated_residual = score_components(public_training_inputs)\nassert base + gated_residual reconstructs original raw scores\nfor alpha in alpha_grid:\n    configure score = base + alpha * gated_residual\n    write trial-local predictor and complete restoration metadata\n    verify reload/export scores, rankings, routing, batching, and candidate permutation\n    obtain pinned host validation HR@10 for all four categories\n    record category HR@10, equal-weight macro HR@10, availability of paired diagnostics, and costs\nselected_alpha = argmax((macro_HR10[alpha], alpha))\nexport selected predictor and matching diagnostic configuration\nverify final export parity\nrecord all comparisons and descriptive twenty-user full-catalog lists\n\nrun_full_diagnosis(train_loader):\n    saved_flags = capture_each_submodule_training_flag(view.model)\n    try:\n        view.model.eval()\n        with no_grad():\n            measure existing pairwise and geometry probes\n            bound work to four batches and 512 valid positions per batch\n            measure branch sensitivity only when scoring context is available\n            attach counts, definitions, units, signs, missing reasons, and limitations\n        return {'metrics': metrics, 'metric_definitions': definitions}\n    finally:\n        restore_each_submodule_training_flag(saved_flags)\n\ndiagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis):\n    embed measured metrics and definitions\n    request interpretation grounded in those observations\n    distinguish training fit, inference sensitivity, validation, and unknown preferences",
    "originality": {
      "score": 3,
      "positive": "Connects measured coverage and train/deployment discrepancies to an intervention on the exact deployed candidate-dependent branch.",
      "negative": "Scalar branch ablation and calibration are established; verification and careful interpretation strengthen reliability rather than algorithmic novelty."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 6,
      "positive": "Can cheaply establish whether transition support, graph alignment, or residual training merits further investigation when the branch exists.",
      "negative": "Applicability is conditional, and scalar calibration cannot recover missing evidence, establish current intent, or resolve co-adaptation and full-catalog relevance."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "Requires no extra training when a valid checkpoint and explicit deployed residual hook already exist.",
      "negative": "History-aware score decomposition, graph restoration, compatibility adapters, and predictor parity require more work than a simple scalar change; a branch-free SASRec cannot run the experiment as proposed."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "Dallmann et al.: sampled evaluation of sequential recommendation",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.13045",
      "contributions": [
        "Reports disagreement between sampled-candidate and full-catalog model comparisons.",
        "Motivates separating pinned benchmark selection from descriptive full-catalog diagnostics."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish that residual calibration improves this benchmark.",
        "Does not justify replacing the pinned evaluation protocol."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Walk the Talk? — ICLR 2025",
      "link": "https://proceedings.iclr.cc/paper_files/paper/2025/hash/b5ec50eb177908f21f78ed0d76ed525c-Abstract-Conference.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Provides evidence that plausible explanations can misrepresent influential recommendation evidence.",
        "Supports directly testing model influence rather than treating narrative plausibility as causal evidence."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not supply current-intent or compatibility labels for these users.",
        "Does not validate the proposed residual scale selection rule."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "A Framework and Toolkit for Testing the Correctness of Recommendation Algorithms — Michiels et al., ACM Transactions on Recommender Systems, 2024",
      "link": "https://doi.org/10.1145/3591109",
      "contributions": [
        "Adapts metamorphic testing, property-based testing, and coverage analysis to recommendation algorithms.",
        "Provides the methodological basis for architecture-aware correctness checks across unit, integration, and system levels.",
        "The released RecPack Tests toolkit is available at https://gitlab.com/recpack-maintainers/recpack-tests."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Implementation correctness does not establish recommendation quality.",
        "The proposed score reconstruction, export parity, routing, and causal-mask checks are task-specific adaptations, not verified built-in toolkit checks."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Official SASRec model implementation",
      "link": "https://github.com/kang205/SASRec/blob/master/model.py",
      "contributions": [
        "Shows candidate scoring by dot product with the final sequence representation using the shared item embedding table.",
        "Returns raw prediction logits and contains no auxiliary transition-graph scoring branch.",
        "Establishes why the residual-bearing architecture prerequisite must be verified locally."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "The reference implementation does not establish the contents of the local RecHarness model or checkpoint.",
        "Its original hardcoded 101-candidate interface requires adaptation; it does not match this benchmark's 100-candidate evaluation directly."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "BestowGNN — out-of-fold graph stacking",
      "link": "https://openreview.net/pdf?id=LO02YHxrxd",
      "contributions": [
        "Uses out-of-fold predictions to address leakage and mismatch in stacked graph learning.",
        "Motivates inspecting how training graph features exclude the examples or users being supervised."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Whole-user exclusion for transition graphs is a methodological adaptation, not an established result for this task.",
        "Cross-fitting does not guarantee matching graph support, normalization, or residual scale at deployment."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Ji et al.: unavailable-interaction leakage in recommendation evaluation",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.11060",
      "contributions": [
        "Shows that unavailable-interaction leakage can affect recommendation comparisons.",
        "Supports keeping validation targets out of graph construction and preserving benchmark splits."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not validate this alpha grid or establish transition-branch utility.",
        "Leakage prevention alone does not eliminate shortcut learning or adaptive validation overfitting."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
